playable.evidence:
The rules cover every case:
- the deck and its composition;
- the hidden and public collections;
- the private A/B view and choice;
- the public lot;
- simultaneous talk;
- sealed bids from 0 up to the gold held;
- an explicit tie rule (tied high bid: nobody pays, lot discarded) and an explicit 0-bid rule;
- what happens to colour and Church lots;
- the secret mark, asked every round and counting only if the kept card was a Church (a single conditional, which the engine supports);
- public Church changes with bounds;
- income;
- the final-mark order with clamping;
- majorities with a half-worth tie rounded down;
- gold in blocks of 5;
- the winner, with a draw on a tie.

The game is finite at 12 rounds and uses 24 of 36 cards, so the deck never runs out. The result is arithmetic on recorded numbers.

Limits: 12 steps per round (at most 20). 4 choice steps per round (keep, talk, bid, mark/Church; at most 4). 18 attributes (at most 20; plausible for 5 worths, 3 gold totals, hidden and public collections, deck, lot and round). 2 hidden elements: deck order and the hidden collections, including marks (at most 3). 0 random draws per round, since there is one shuffle at setup and drawing is removal (at most 1). 12 rounds (at most 40).

Scoring majorities among 3 players needs calc steps (the arithmetic_ranking workaround), but this is a one-time end phase. The game needs no board, judge or clock.

TALK. Talk opens each round, before the bid. It is simultaneous: all write, then all are shown. It fits the conflict: players compete for the same lot, a proposal such as 'I'll bid 0, you bid 2 against the leader' costs whoever follows it, and the tie rule gives a reason to signal or bluff. It is not a case where everyone copies the first speaker. No unsupported features (trading, binding deals, blocks of conditional steps) are used.
